{%- comment -%}
  Shop-wide reviews — the real ones.

  Usage:
    {% render 'shop-reviews', show: 'summary' %}   average + count
    {% render 'shop-reviews', show: 'carousel' %}  the review cards

  ── What this replaces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Six customer testimonials written into assets/app.js — names, emirates,
  the products they had supposedly bought, five stars each and a "Verified
  Purchase" tick — rendered on ten surfaces including every collection page.
  Alongside them, "4.8, based on 10,284 verified reviews" typed into two
  editorial sections.

  None of it described anyone. It came from the static build the theme was
  ported from, where it was placeholder copy; products and brands were both
  switched to live data during that port and the testimonials were missed.

  Invented reviews are the one piece of social proof that is worth less than
  nothing: a store that fabricates them is making an unsubstantiated claim
  about real named people, and a shopper who recognises one stops believing
  the authenticity guarantee too — which is this store's whole proposition.

  ── What it does instead ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Reads Judge.me, which already holds the store's real reviews and already
  renders them on the product page. Nothing is rendered unless Judge.me
  reports at least one shop review, and the caller is told so through
  data-reviews-empty, so a heading never sits above an empty space.

  A store with no shop reviews yet therefore shows no reviews section. That
  is the correct state: it is also the thing to fix, by turning on Judge.me's
  shop-review requests — see docs/judgeme-review-requests.md.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # all_reviews_*, not shop_reviews_*. Judge.me publishes both: shop_reviews
  # counts only reviews about the shop itself (3), all_reviews counts those
  # plus every product review (24). The carousel below shows all of them, so
  # the summary has to count the same set or the two contradict each other on
  # the same screen.
  assign sr_count = shop.metafields.judgeme.all_reviews_count | plus: 0
  assign sr_rating = shop.metafields.judgeme.all_reviews_rating
  assign sr_mode = show | default: 'carousel'
-%}

{%- if sr_count > 0 -%}
  {%- if sr_mode == 'summary' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Stars only when there is an average behind them. A count on its own is
      still true; five stars on nothing is the thing this file exists to
      remove.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="rv-summary fade-up">
      {%- if sr_rating != blank -%}
        <div class="rv-big">{{ sr_rating | times: 1.0 | round: 1 }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}
      <div class="rv-details">
        {%- if sr_rating != blank -%}
          {% render 'star-rating', rating: sr_rating %}
        {%- endif -%}
        <p>Based on {{ sr_count }} verified review{% unless sr_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The metafield, not an empty container.

      The first version of this rendered <div class="jdgm-carousel"></div> and
      trusted Judge.me's script to find and fill it. It never did: the heading
      shipped over an empty box, which is the exact failure this snippet was
      written to prevent, just with the fabricated reviews taken out.

      judgeme.featured_carousel holds the widget already rendered — six real
      reviews, about 4 KB of HTML, written by the app whenever reviews change.
      Server-rendered means it is there at first paint, needs no round trip,
      and cannot shift the layout when a script arrives late.

      The wrapper div is Judge.me's own documented requirement; the widget
      sizes its slides against it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div style="clear:both"></div>
    <div class="jdgm-carousel-wrapper">
      {{ shop.metafields.judgeme.featured_carousel }}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Nothing real to show. The marker lets the caller drop its own heading
    rather than leaving "What Customers Say" above a gap.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <span hidden data-reviews-empty></span>
{%- endif -%}
